screens.prose:
In Python you grow a list with `append`. A `std::vector<double>` is C++'s growing list, with one fixed element type.

```cpp
std::vector<double> closes;
closes.push_back(101.5);
closes.push_back(102.0);
closes.push_back(100.8);
std::cout << closes.size() << ' '
          << closes[1] << '\n';
```

`size()` counts the elements in use; `capacity()` counts the slots in the block. A full block doubles on the next `push_back`. `reserve(n)` asks for room for `n` up front.

```cpp
std::vector<double> ticks;
ticks.reserve(1000);
for (int i = 0; i < 600; ++i) {
    ticks.push_back(100.0 + i * 0.01);
}
```

