screens.prose:
A feed sends zeros for missing ticks. This loop tries to drop them.

```cpp
std::vector<double> ticks{101.2, 0.0, 0.0, 101.5};
for (auto it = ticks.begin(); it != ticks.end(); ++it) {
    if (*it == 0.0) {
        ticks.erase(it);
    }
}
```

explain.text:
`erase` shifts the later elements left and leaves `it` invalid: using it again is undefined behaviour. Here it skipped a zero; at the last element it can run past `end()`. Python lists skip the same way. The fix takes the iterator `erase` returns:

```cpp
for (auto it = ticks.begin(); it != ticks.end(); ) {
    if (*it == 0.0) it = ticks.erase(it);
    else ++it;
}
```

